---
/**
 * About page.
 *
 * This page exists to make three things impossible to misunderstand:
 *   1. This site is not Texas A&M and has nothing to do with Texas A&M.
 *   2. The pay figures are a machine's reading of free text and can be wrong.
 *   3. Profile data is local-first and enters only the signed-in private vault.
 *
 * The snapshot timestamp and study counts are read from the build data rather
 * than written by hand, so the freshness claim on this page cannot drift away
 * from what the site is actually serving.
 */
import snapshotJson from '@/data/studies.json';
import Base from '@/layouts/Base.astro';
import { url } from '@/lib/view.ts';
import { hydrateStudiesSnapshot } from '@/studies/hydrate.ts';
import { studyHasDisplaySource } from '@/studies/source-display.ts';
import { CLINICAL_TRIALS_API_URL } from '@/studies/sources/clinicaltrials.ts';
import type { Snapshot } from '@/studies/types.ts';
import { STUDY_API_URL } from '@/studies/types.ts';

const snapshot = hydrateStudiesSnapshot(snapshotJson as unknown as Snapshot);

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/*$/, '/');

const fetchedAt = new Date(snapshot.fetchedAt);
const fetchedLabel = Number.isNaN(fetchedAt.getTime())
  ? 'unknown'
  : fetchedAt.toLocaleString('en-US', {
      dateStyle: 'long',
      timeStyle: 'short',
      timeZone: 'UTC',
    }) + ' UTC';

const total = snapshot.studies.length;
const expired = snapshot.studies.filter((s) => s.isExpired).length;
const rated = snapshot.studies.filter((s) => s.effectiveHourly !== null).length;
const arvCount = snapshot.studies.filter((s) => studyHasDisplaySource(s, 'aggie-research-volunteers')).length;
const clinicalCount = snapshot.studies.filter((s) => studyHasDisplaySource(s, 'clinicaltrials-gov')).length;
---

<Base
  title="About studies — Radar"
  description="An unofficial index of Texas A&M participant-study opportunities from Aggie Research Volunteers and ClinicalTrials.gov."
  engine="studies"
  fetchedAt={snapshot.fetchedAt}
  current="studies"
  layout="wide"
>
  <article class="studies-app studies-about prose">
      <nav class="mb-6 text-sm">
        <a href={url(base, 'studies/')}>
          &larr; All studies
        </a>
      </nav>

      <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold sm:text-3xl">About this site</h1>

      <!-- The disclaimer goes first, not in the footer. -->
      <div class="mt-6 rounded-lg border-2 border-gray-400 bg-white p-4">
        <p class="font-semibold">
          Not affiliated with, endorsed by, sponsored by, or operated by Texas A&amp;M University.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-700">
          This is an independent, unofficial project built by one person. It reuses public data
          from Texas A&amp;M and ClinicalTrials.gov. The university has not reviewed it, has
          no involvement in it, and is not responsible for anything on it. "Texas A&amp;M",
          "Aggie", and related names belong to the university and are used here only to describe
          whose listings these are.
        </p>
      </div>

      <section class="mt-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-bold">What this is</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Texas A&amp;M runs a public board of research studies looking for volunteers, many of
          which pay. The official board is a good list but a hard one to compare: pay is written as
          free text ("Up to $30, paid as an Amazon gift card"), time commitment is written as
          different free text ("One-session study lasting about 2 hours"), and the two are never
          put together. So there is no way to see which studies are actually worth your afternoon.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Radar also checks ClinicalTrials.gov for recruiting studies led by Texas A&amp;M or with
          a recruiting Texas A&amp;M site. It combines exact protocol or exact-title matches, keeps
          the richer ARV pay details, and lists registry-only opportunities with pay honestly marked
          unknown. It also flags official records that look old or have passed their stated end date.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-bold">Where the data comes from</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          The first source is the public Aggie Research Volunteers WordPress API at
          <a class="break-all text-brand-700 underline" href={STUDY_API_URL} rel="noopener external">
            {STUDY_API_URL}
          </a>, plus the four matching taxonomy endpoints for category, location, session type, and
          topic. No login, no scraping of pages, no private endpoints — it is the same JSON the
          university's own study board is built from.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          The second is the official ClinicalTrials.gov API at
          <a class="break-all text-brand-700 underline" href={CLINICAL_TRIALS_API_URL} rel="noopener external">
            {CLINICAL_TRIALS_API_URL}
          </a>. Radar asks for recruiting trials whose lead sponsor is exactly Texas A&amp;M University,
          plus externally sponsored trials where a named Texas A&amp;M facility is itself recruiting.
          Broad search results are post-filtered so collaborators and closed sites do not leak in.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          The sources are fetched server-side and baked into static files; the public feed never
          calls either registry from your browser. At runtime, only an explicit private-vault
          sign-in talks to Firebase.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-bold">How often it refreshes</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          An automated refresh checks both registries twice daily and deploys a new snapshot when
          the material data changes. There is no live browser connection — what you are reading is
          the timestamped snapshot below. If one registry is unavailable or incomplete, Radar keeps
          that source&rsquo;s last successful records instead of treating the outage as mass deletion.
        </p>
        <dl class="mt-3 rounded-lg border border-gray-300 bg-white p-4 text-sm">
          <div><dt class="inline font-semibold">Snapshot taken:</dt><dd class="inline"> {fetchedLabel}</dd></div>
          <div class="mt-1"><dt class="inline font-semibold">Studies listed:</dt><dd class="inline"> {total}</dd></div>
          <div class="mt-1"><dt class="inline font-semibold">Confirmed by Aggie Research Volunteers:</dt><dd class="inline"> {arvCount}</dd></div>
          <div class="mt-1"><dt class="inline font-semibold">Confirmed by ClinicalTrials.gov:</dt><dd class="inline"> {clinicalCount}</dd></div>
          <div class="mt-1"><dt class="inline font-semibold">Already past their end date:</dt><dd class="inline"> {expired}</dd></div>
          <div class="mt-1"><dt class="inline font-semibold">With a computable $/hour:</dt><dd class="inline"> {rated} of {total}</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-700">
          Studies also come and go upstream faster than a snapshot can track. A listing that was
          live when the snapshot was taken may have closed since, which is why every study page
          leads with "ask whether they are still recruiting".
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-bold">The pay figures are best-effort</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          This is the most important caveat on the site. The dollar and hour figures are produced
          by a heuristic parser reading ARV sentences written by dozens of different labs with no
          shared format. ClinicalTrials.gov does not publish participant compensation or duration,
          so Radar never invents either for registry-only records. The parser handles common ARV
          shapes well and it definitely gets some listings wrong.
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
          <li>
            <strong>The official listing is authoritative.</strong> Every study page links to it
            prominently, and shows the exact compensation and duration text under "Source text" so
            you can check our arithmetic in one click.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Guaranteed money only.</strong> A raffle entry is never counted as pay, no
            matter how large the prize, because the expected value of a drawing is not a wage.
            Raffles are shown separately.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>"Unknown" is not "unpaid".</strong> When pay or time cannot be read honestly,
            the rate is left blank and sorts last, rather than being recorded as $0.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Nothing here is negotiated or promised.</strong> What you are actually paid is
            between you and the study team.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-bold">The eligibility filter is a hint</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          If you fill in the optional details form, this site compares your answers with the
          criteria it read out of each listing and sorts studies into "looks like a fit",
          "probably not", and "cannot tell". That is
          <strong>a reading of some wording on a web page, not a medical or official
          determination</strong>, and it is not medical advice. Only the study team can decide who
          is eligible, and they will screen you themselves.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Anything you leave blank counts as "not answered", never as "no". That deliberately keeps
          more studies visible rather than fewer: quietly filtering out a study someone would have
          qualified for is a worse mistake than showing one they will not.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-bold">Your details are private and local-first</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          The optional form asks about age, handedness, MRI safety, cardiovascular conditions,
          pregnancy, seizure history, sex, fasting, and student status. Those answers are stored in
          your browser's local storage. If you explicitly sign in with either provisioned Google
          account, the same private owner-vault record follows you across devices.
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
          <li>The public study index is static. Firebase stores only the signed-in owner's private profile and study preferences.</li>
          <li>There is no analytics, no tracking pixel, and no third-party script on any page.</li>
          <li>
            Contact drafts are still created only in your mail client, and you see the full text
            before you send it.
          </li>
          <li>"Clear my details" clears the profile locally and updates the synced private record.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-bold">About the coordinators' email addresses</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          ARV listings usually include a contact address, often a graduate student's personal university
          email. Those people agreed to be listed on a university recruitment page; they did not
          agree to be on a scrapeable mirror. So no address appears in this site's HTML — each one
          is encoded and assembled in your browser only after you press "Show contact details".
          That stops bulk harvesters, not people, which is the point. ClinicalTrials.gov contact
          names, addresses, and phone numbers are not ingested at all; its official record is linked.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          The same applies to the downloadable dataset: <code>api/studies.json</code> publishes
          every parsed figure but blanks out the contact name, email and phone, because one file
          holding all of them is exactly the list this is meant to prevent. The build refuses to
          finish if an address ever reaches either the pages or the dataset.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          If you are a coordinator and would rather your study not appear here at all, that is a
          fair ask and it will be honoured.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-bold">Known limitations</h2>
        <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
          <li>
            Some listings genuinely enrol minors — the minimum age goes as low as 2. Nothing here
            assumes an 18+ audience, so use the age field.
          </li>
          <li>
            A stated maximum age of 100 or 125 is a data-entry placeholder for "no limit", and is
            treated as no upper bound rather than as a real cutoff.
          </li>
          <li>
            The same protocol is sometimes posted more than once under the same IRB number. Those
            are collapsed to the most recently updated copy, so a study you saw upstream may appear
            here only once.
          </li>
          <li>
            Cross-registry matching uses a normalized protocol identifier first and an exact title
            second. Radar never fuzzy-merges two studies just because their titles sound similar.
          </li>
          <li>
            The upstream <code>status</code> field says "publish" even for listings whose
            recruitment end date has passed, so expiry is worked out from the dates instead.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <footer class="mt-10 border-t border-gray-300 pt-4 text-xs text-gray-600">
        <p>
          Unofficial and independent. Not affiliated with or endorsed by Texas A&amp;M University.
          Underlying study data comes from Texas A&amp;M, ClinicalTrials.gov, and the individual
          research teams.
        </p>
      </footer>
      </article>
</Base>
